<!--
    An inline system call (`ecall`) lifts to a CALLOTHER with no register inputs
    and no output, so the a0 a function sets up for it still holds that value
    afterwards. `pair` returns the two argument registers it set up as a register pair, and
    `after` doubles its argument: it reads back the a0 it saved, not the result.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 flow-classification.
    P0 assertion:      option syscallregs off|auto|on  (default auto).

    Source (clang -O2 -target riscv64-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes verbatim below):

        long pair(long a, long b) {
          register long a0 asm("a0") = b; register long a1 asm("a1") = a;
          register long a7 asm("a7") = 64;
          asm volatile("ecall" : "+r"(a0) : "r"(a1), "r"(a7) : "memory");
          return a0;
        }
        long after(long x) {
          register long a0 asm("a0"); register long a7 asm("a7") = 172;
          asm volatile("ecall" : "=r"(a0) : "r"(a7) : "memory");
          if (a0 < 0) return -1;
          return a0 + x;
        }
        long fence(long a) { asm volatile("fence.i" ::: "memory"); return a; }
        long retry(long fd, char *buf, long n) {
          long r;
          do {
            register long x0 asm("a0") = fd; register char *x1 asm("a1") = buf;
            register long x2 asm("a2") = n; register long x8 asm("a7") = 63;
            asm volatile("ecall" : "+r"(x0) : "r"(x1), "r"(x2), "r"(x8) : "memory");
            r = x0;
          } while (r == -4);
          return r;
        }

    Pass 1 (option syscallregs on) gives the ecall its effects: it writes a0,
    and it reads the number register a7 and then the argument registers in
    order, up to the first one the function did not write on every path to
    it.  Pass 2 (option syscallregs off) is the old model, and so is pass 3,
    the default `auto`: it acts only on an image the loader identified as a
    program for an operating system's user space, which a bare byte chunk is
    not.  fence is the control: its barrier is a CALLOTHER too,
    and nothing about it changes.  retry hands buf and n to the kernel in a1
    and a2 untouched, and the compiler keeps fd in a3 and the constant -4 in
    a4: the call reads a7 and a0, and neither the untouched registers nor the
    scratch ones after them take an argument position.
-->
